<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Turki ibn Abdullah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1755–1834</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Turki ibn Abdullah Al Saud</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Turki ibn Abdullah was the founder of the second Saudi state, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Nejd</a>. A grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-saud">Muhammad ibn Saud</a>, he survived the Ottoman-Egyptian destruction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diriyah">Diriyah</a> in 1818 and in 1824 drove the Egyptian garrison from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riyadh">Riyadh</a>, which he made his capital. He restored Saudi rule over Najd and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-ahsa-oasis">al-Hasa</a> and renewed the alliance with the Wahhabi scholars, but was assassinated by a cousin in 1834.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/turki-ibn-abdullah.jpg" alt="Turki ibn Abdullah" width="293" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: محمد المهداوي · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A5%D9%85%D8%A7%D9%85_%D8%AA%D8%B1%D9%83%D9%8A_%D8%A8%D9%86_%D8%B9%D8%A8%D8%AF_%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%84%D9%87_%D8%A2%D9%84_%D8%B3%D8%B9%D9%88%D8%AF.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
